 Flag\n改语言，保持原画面意思不变\n![Image](img_v3_0215a_f9165d00-9d35-4d6f-9fed-2eebc3103d9g)\n**设计稿件：**\n![Image](img_v3_0215a_d4102056-d468-46e9-80e1-0b9d4eb32a2g)\n![Image](img_v3_0215a_3b5953ce-a05b-4f2f-b67d-3b534e7b8abg)\n![Image](img_v3_0215a_48824f3b-50be-42cb-87f0-6212d39784eg)\n![Image](img_v3_0215a_ef88bbd0-6b06-47f1-a50e-2945334074cg)\n![Image](img_v3_0215a_0dce5508-bc73-46b1-97ef-aac674a6215g)\n\nこんな症状に注意\n安心して、ゆっ\nくり呼吸を。\n次のような様子なら、自宅で見守りましょう：\n安心できるサイン\n鼻水が出ていても、食事や睡眠がとれてい\nて、笑顔が見られる。\n24時間で4〜6回以上、おむつがしっかり濡\nれている。\n眠っている間も落ち着いていて、呼吸が安定\nしている（息をあえぐような様子がない）。\n自宅でできるケア\n1. 生理食塩水の点鼻\n2. 冷たいミストの加湿器\n3起きている間は、上体を起こして抱っこする\n##: AAP HealthyChildren, Penn Medicine, Sanford Health, AAP Safe Sleep Guidelines\nmomcozy\n>›>>\n\nmomcozy\nmomcozy\nmomcozy\nNo fever. Still congested.\nDo I take them in?\nIt's 2 AM. You're staring at the crib. They sound\nawful. The thermometer says they're fine.\nYou're torn: \"Do I risk the waiting room germs,\nor am I waiting too long?\"\nFever is only half the story.\nA normal thermometer doesn't always mean\n\"all clear.\" But it's not a reason to panic either.\nWatch how they're breathing and acting -\nnot just the number.\nSource: AAP HealthyChildren (healthychildren.org / Children & Colds)\nHow long is too long?\nmomcozy\nBREATHE EASY.\nWatch at home if:\nMost infant colds fade in 7-10 days\n(though congestion can linger).\nWatch For\nThe Safe Sign\nEating, sleeping, and smiling between sniffles.\n4-6+ heavy wet diapers in 24 hours.\nRelaxed and steady while asleep (no gasping).\nWatch For\nSwipe →\nDay1\nOnset\nDay 7-10\nDay14\nMost Common Colds Improve\nStill Not Better?\nCall the doctor if:\n• Symptoms last 10-14+ days without improving.\n• They get better, then suddenly get worse.\nThe Bottom Line\nTreat the baby, not the thermometer. You know them best.\nSwipe →\nSource: AAD HealthvChildren / Penn Medicine / Sanford Health\nmomcozy\nDON'T WAIT.\nCall the doctor if:\nThe Red Flag\nUnder 3 months old with any cold signs.\nWorking hard