resize before you turn suction up.\n\n02\n\nLeak-proofing\n\nCheck how the cup sits in a supportive pumping bra and whether you can lean forward a little. A poor seal is often fit or angle, not a reason to jump model.\n\n03\n\nHow slim it sits\n\nAir 1 is the slimmest all-in-one at 61 mm. Choose that lower profile if you’re packing\nbreastfeeding essentials for working mums\nand need the cup to sit flatter under work clothes.\n\n04\n\nCleaning\n\nCount the parts that touch milk and how quickly you can put the cup back together. A heated cup has extra parts. A wearable you dread washing will not stay your everyday pump.\n\n05\n\nWarmth and massage\n\nWellness 1 warms the cup to 37°C, 39°C or 41°C and adds massage with the suction rhythm. Warmth uses more charge — about 6 sessions with warmth, about 10 pumping only.\n\nIf you’re still\nchoosing a breast pump in the UK\n, start with format: a tube-free all-in-one wearable, or a separate unit with in-bra collection cups. Then pick a model from Best for, or V1 Pro / V2 Pro above.\n\nFrequently asked questions\n\nWearable pump FAQs\n\nWhich wearable breast pump is best?\n\nThere isn’t one best wearable breast pump. For warmth and massage, start with Wellness 1. For everyday pumping, M5 Smart. For\nworking mums\nwho need a slimmer in-bra profile, Air 1. For app control and a quieter motor, M9. M6 skips the app. S12 Pro Quick covers a lower price. Match the job rather than chasing a single winner.\n\nWhat is a hands-free breast pump?\n\nHands-free means you are not holding the flanges. That can be an all-in-one wearable, or a separate unit with in-bra collection cups. Hands-free is not a product type on its own.\n\nAre V1 Pro and V2 Pro wearable pumps?\n\nYes. In the UK, wearable usually means the cups sit in your bra. V1 Pro and V2 Pro do that with a separate motor unit and in-bra collection cups. They are not all-in-one, tube-free wearables like M5 Smart or Air 1.\nShop hospital-strength electric pumps\nif you want that format in more depth.\n\nHow should I store expressed milk in the UK?\n\nFollow current NHS guidance. The NHS advises storing expressed milk in the fridge for up to 8 days at 4°C or lower, and in a freezer at −18°C or lower for up to 6 months. Some NHS pages also allow up to 6 hours at room temperature. Check the\nNHS expressing page\nbefore you rely on a figure.\n\nWhat flange size do I need?\n\nMeasure nipple diameter, not bra cup. Most Momcozy wearables include 17/19/21 mm inserts with a 24 mm flange. Pain, a white ring on the nipple, or too much areola being pulled in means resize before you turn suction up. Fit matters more than buying a “stronger” pump.\n\nDo wearable pumps decrease milk supply?\n\nNot if the cup empties the breast well. Supply drops when the seal is poor, the flange is the wrong size, or sessions are too short or too infrequent — not because the motor sits in the bra. If output falls, check fit first, then session length. Speak to a midwife or lactation specialist if you’re worried about supply.\n\nCan I exclusively use a wearable breast pump?\n\nYes, if it empties you comfortably and your output stays steady. Plenty of UK mums use an all-in-one wearable as their only pump. If you need longer sessions at home with the motor off the chest, V1 Pro and V2 Pro are on this page; the wider\nhospital-strength electric\nrange is on the Electric collection.\n\nCan I wear a regular bra while pumping?\n\nA stretchy everyday bra can hold a cup if it is supportive enough to keep the seal. A dedicated pumping bra is more reliable for leaks and for leaning forward a little. If milk escapes, it is usually fit or angle, not a reason to jump model.\n\nDoes warmth and massage in a wearable pump help with let-down?\n\nSome mums find in-cup warmth and a massage rhythm easier at the start of a session. Wellness 1 warms to 37°C, 39°C or 41°C. Warmth uses more battery — about 6 sessions with warmth, about 10 pumping only. It is a comfort feature, not a medical treatm