igger a faster let-down and help you collect more milk in a different, more comfortable way.\n\n7. How do I know if my breast pump flange fits correctly?\n\nA good\nflange fit\nmeans your nipple moves freely in the tunnel without rubbing against the sides. If you see redness, feel pain, or notice that your nipple is being pulled too far into the pump, you might need a different size. Using the right size helps you get more milk and keeps you comfortable.\n\n8. Should pumping ever be painful?\n\nPumping should never hurt. If you feel pain, try lowering the suction level. More suction does not always mean more milk! You should also check your fit and try using warmth or massage—like the features found on the Momcozy W1—to make the session feel more natural.\n\n9. How often should I replace my breast pump parts?\n\nIt is a good idea to check\npump parts\nlike the silicone valves and membranes every few weeks. If they look worn out, stretched, or torn, replace them with new ones. Fresh parts help the pump keep its strong suction so you can collect more milk.\n\n10. How do I clean and maintain my breast pump?\n\nKeeping your breast pump clean\nis very important for your health and your baby's safety. After every use, take apart all the pieces that touched your milk. Wash them well with warm, soapy water. Rinse them off and let them air dry completely before you put the pump back together. You can also wipe the main pump motor with a damp cloth to keep it clean. Taking good care of your pump helps it last longer and keeps germs away.\n\n11. Can I move around while I pump?\n\nYes! Using a wearable pump like the Momcozy W1 or Air 1 allows you to stay active. Just make sure the pump is tucked securely in your bra so it doesn't shift, which ensures the fit stays correct and the suction remains steady while you move.\n\nConclusion\n\nOverall, it is not your entire fault if you have a low milk supply, but it is your responsibility as a mother to give your baby\nthe best possibl