ay.\n\nMost electric kits are doubles, so you can express both sides in one session and cut the time. If feeding isn’t going well, speak to your midwife or health visitor.\n\nAfter you pump\n\nBags, bras and a bottle washer\n\nOnce you switch the motor off, you still need to store the milk, keep the cups in place, and wash the parts.\n\nStore\n\nMilk storage\n\nUse breast milk storage bags to label and freeze milk flat. Follow NHS guidance on how long milk keeps. A storage jug is useful at home if you prefer to pour milk together before you bag it.\n\nHold\n\nPumping bras\n\nA pumping bra holds the cups in place so your hands are free while you pump. An ordinary stretchy bra can work; a pumping bra is more likely to stay put.\n\nClean\n\nBottle washer kits\n\nA bottle washer washes and sterilises pump parts in one go, so you’re not facing a sink of parts after every session. It is faster than\ncleaning breast pump parts\nby hand. Add a washing block if you want the detergent measured for you.\n\nBefore you buy\n\nWhat to check on an electric breast pump\n\n01\n\nIn the bra, or outside?\n\nIf you want the whole pump in your bra with no tubes, start with M5 Smart. Pick Air 1 if you need a slimmer cup, Wellness 1 if you want in-cup warmth and massage, or M6 if you’d rather skip an app. If you’d rather keep the motor off your chest, choose V1 Pro, or V2 Pro for a lighter unit.\n\n02\n\nA comfortable fit\n\nSize the cup to your nipple, not your bra size. Most kits include a 24 mm flange and 17, 19 and 21 mm inserts. If pumping hurts, you see a white ring, or too much breast is pulled in, try another size before you turn the suction up.\n\n03\n\nCharging and battery\n\nThese pumps charge with USB-C, like a phone. Top up from a wall plug, a laptop or a power bank, then pump on battery at home, at work or out. If you’ll be away from a charger for several sessions, pick one that lasts longer on a charge. Warmth uses more battery.\n\n04\n\nOne side or both\n\nIf you pump both sides most days, a double kit is quicker. Only buy a single if you usually express one side, or you want a spare.\n\nWhen you know which type you want, check suction, battery and\nbreast pump price\n.\n\nFrequently asked questions\n\nElectric breast pump FAQs\n\nWhat is an electric breast pump?\n\nAn electric breast pump uses a motor to express milk, so you’re not squeezing a handle. You can get ones that sit fully in your bra with no tubes, or stronger pumps where the cups go in your bra and the motor stays outside.\n\nWhich electric breast pump is best?\n\nThere isn’t one best pump for every mum. Most people start with M5 Smart for everyday use. Choose Air 1 if you want a slimmer cup and a charging case. Choose Wellness 1 if you want in-cup warmth and massage. Choose V1 Pro if you want a stronger motor off your chest.\n\nWhat’s the difference between the two types of electric pump?\n\nThe difference is where the motor sits. One type has the motor in the cup: no tubes, the whole pump goes in your bra. That’s M5 Smart, Air 1, Wellness 1, M6, M9, S12 Pro Quick and S9 Pro. The other type still puts the cups in your bra, but the motor sits outside on a short tube. That’s V1 Pro and V2 Pro — hospital-strength, with the motor off your chest. Choose the no-tube type if you want to move around with nothing hanging off you. Choose V1 Pro or V2 Pro if you’d rather keep a stronger motor off your chest.\n\nAre V1 Pro and V2 Pro wearable pumps?\n\nYes. In the UK, wearable usually means the cups sit in your bra so you can move around. V1 Pro and V2 Pro do that — the motor just sits outside, with tubing. They are not the tube-free, all-in-one kind.\n\nIs a portable or wireless breast pump the same as a wearable?\n\nNo. Portable or wireless just means it runs on battery, so you’re not plugged into the wall while you pump. Wearable means the cups sit in your bra. Most of these pumps are both: cordless, with the cups in the bra.\n\nShould I get an electric or a manual pump?\n\nGo electric if you pump most days, you’re going back to work, or you want both sides done in one go. A manual pump is fine if you only express now and then, and you want something quiet with no charging.\n\nCan I pump both sides at once?\n\nYes. Most electric kits do both sides together, which is the usual choice if you express from both breasts. A few are also sold as a single — only pick that if you usually pump one side, or you want a spare.\n\nDo I need a pumping bra or storage bags?\n\nYou can pump without them, but most mums who express every day end up wanting both. A pumping bra holds the cups so your hands are free. Bags let you label and freeze milk for later.\n\nHow should I store expressed milk?\n\nFollow the NHS. They currently say fridge for up to 8 days at 4°C or lower, and freezer at −18°C or lower for up to 6 months. Some NHS pages also allow up to 6 hours at room temperature. Check the\nNHS expressing page\nfor the latest.\n\nCan an electric pump help with engorgement?\n\nIt can take the pressure off if you feel uncomfortably full. If you think you have mastitis, feeding hurts, or baby is not getting milk, talk to your midwife or health visitor.\n\nFAQs\n\nGlobal No.1 Wearable Breast Pump Brand*\n\n*According to Grand View Research's Wearable Breast Pump Market Analysis and Segment Forecasts to 2030 (2024), Momcozy ranked No.1 globally in the Wearable Breast Pump category based on total market share in 2024. Rankings are based on the methodology and market definition used in the report.\n\nOUR LATEST ACTIVITIES\n\nJOIN IN\nOUR NEWSLETTER\n\nBe among the first to receive updates on our latest products, product innovations, and exclusive offers.\n\nSubscribe\n\nSign up now for sale alerts & special offers.\n\nGet in touch\n\nROOT INTERNATIONAL LIMITED.\nSuite 302, 4 Station Square, Cambridge,\n\nCB1 2GE, United Kingdom\n\nPhone：\n\nUK: +44 845 351 1671\n\n(Calls cost Xp plus your phone company's access charge)\n\nUS: +1 619 848 0676\n\nFR: +33 805 980 33