em using pre-tax dollars.\nCan I get a Momcozy breast pump through insurance?\nYes ! We are proud to have partnered with leading Durable Medical Equipment (DME) providers to help you get a Momcozy breast pump at little to no cost through your insurance. Thanks to the Affordable Care Act (ACA), most health insurance plans are required to cover the cost of a breast pump.\nVerify Coverage : Contact your insurance provider to confirm your breast pump benefits .\nObtain a Prescription : Get a prescription for a breast pump from your OBGYN or healthcare provider.\nChoose a DME Partner : We work with trusted partners like Aeroflow, 1 Natural Way, Babylist Health, and many others. You can visit our insurance page to see the full list. Your chosen DME partner will handle all the paperwork and claims for you.\nHow to choose the right flange size for Momcozy breast pumps?\nThis is the single most important factor for a comfortable and efficient pumping experience. An incorrect flange size is the number one cause of pain and can reduce your milk output.\nThe Golden Rule: Measure your nipple, then add 1-3mm.\nHere is the correct way to measure:\nStimulate : Gently stimulate your nipple so it perks up (do not measure a flat nipple).\nMeasure : Using the nipple ruler included in your Momcozy pump set, find the hole that your nipple fits through snugly without being squeezed. Alternatively, use a ruler to measure the diameter (the width) of the base of your nipple in millimeters (mm). Do not include your areola.\nCalculate : Take your nipple measurement and add 1 to 3 millimeters to find your ideal flange size. For example, if your nipple base measures 21mm, your ideal flange size is 24mm. If your nipple measures 18mm, you should use a 21mm flange insert.\nCheck Both : Your nipples may be different sizes, so be sure to measure both!\nHow to clean Momcozy breast pumps?\nProper hygiene is essential for your baby's health.\nThe Golden Rule : Any part that comes into contact with breas