th a brush used exclusively for infant feeding items.\nRinse again using either running water or clean basin water. Repeat if any residue remains.\nAir dry completely on a clean, unused dish towel or paper towel in a dust-free area. Do not pat dry with clothes, as this may transfer bacteria.\nStep 2: Disinfection (Recommended Once Daily)\nBoil water in a household pot and submerge all previously cleaned parts.\nEnsure no parts are touching the pot sides to avoid heat deformation.\nRemove parts with clean tongs, then air dry thoroughly on a sterile surface.\nDo not disinfect with microwaves or UV sterilizers. High temperatures over 110°C can shorten product lifespan and compromise safety. For proper sterilization methods, please check our article: How to Sterilise a Breast Pump and the Parts .\nStep 3: Cleaning the Motor & Exterior\nAlthough the motor doesn't touch milk, it still needs surface cleaning:\nUse 70% isopropyl alcohol wipes to disinfect.\nLet it air dry for 10 minutes to allow alcohol to evaporate fully.\nNever immerse the motor, cable, or charger in water.\nOnce all parts are clean and dry:\nStore in a clean, closed container or cabinet—not in the open or near sinks.\nIf using a brush or basin regularly, clean and disinfect those as well, especially if your baby is under 3 months, born prematurely, or immunocompromised.\nWhether you're pumping at home or on the go, this simple routine ensures both safety and durability. M6 and M9 are designed to make cleaning feel less like a chore—so you can focus on what matters most.\nBefore using your pump, make sure all milk-contact parts have been properly cleaned and disinfected. Always wash your hands thoroughly before handling pump parts to avoid contamination. Let boiled components cool before assembly to prevent burns.\nWash your hands thoroughly with soap and warm water.\nAttach the diaphragm to the flange. Press gently around the edges to create a secure seal.\nInsert the valve into the flange bottom. Make sure it fits tightly with no visible gaps.\nConnect the flange to the milk collector. Align the outlet and press firmly with your palm for a leak-proof fit.\nAttach the motor to the top of the flange assembly. It should click smoothly into place.\nFor a video demonstration, see this:\nWash your hands thoroughly to maintain hygiene.\nInstall the milk bowl by rotating it into the locked position at the base of the collector. Follow the lock symbol guide.\nAttach the flange to the collector mouth and align it precisely to ensure a vacuum-tight seal.\nMount the main motor unit onto the flange from the top. Press until it connects securely.\nBoth models rely on correct placement of all parts to ma", "unit_id": "8768d73b071d0a35ca79", "unit_type": "clause"}
